## LibreJS - WebExtensions Edition ##


BUILD:

The build depends only on packages provided by the distribution; it needs
neither npm nor network access. On Trisquel 12 / Ubuntu 24.04 install:
  $ sudo apt install node-browserify node-acorn node-hash.js zip

To build the extension plus create a .xpi package run:
  $ ./build.sh

To build the extension including the automated test suite (see TEST below)
you additionally need the Jasmine framework from the distribution:
  $ sudo apt install node-jasmine
  $ ./build.sh -t
  or
  $ ./build.sh --test

The build.sh script sets NODE_PATH to /usr/share/nodejs so that browserify
resolves the distribution's node modules; you do not need a node_modules/
directory. To build the bundle by hand:
  $ NODE_PATH=/usr/share/nodejs browserify main_background.js -o bundle.js

Note: this build.sh script relies on no new source files being created.


DEBUG:

To debug this add-on on IceCat and other Firefox derivatives, browse to the special URL "about:debugging", select "Enable add-on debugging", and load the manifest.json in this directory with the "Load Temporary Add-on" button.

LibreJS should work with other WebExtensions-compliant browsers; but currently, none of them meet the freedom standards of GNU, so no help will be provided for their usage.


TEST:

An automated test suite runs automatically in its own tab whenever the extension
is loaded as a "temporary add-on" from about:debugging.
Otherwise (if included in the xpi, see BUILD above) it can be launched from the
UI by clicking the [Automated self test...] button.


HEADLESS TESTS AND COMPLIANCE CHECK (optional, external tools required):

NOTE: Unlike the rest of LibreJS, the command-line runners in this section
are NOT usable with Trisquel packages alone. They drive a headless browser
through Selenium and require the Node selenium-webdriver library and a
geckodriver binary, neither of which is packaged in Trisquel 12 (which also
does not ship npm). You must obtain these from outside the distribution:
selenium-webdriver e.g. via npm, and geckodriver from
https://github.com/mozilla/geckodriver/releases (ensure geckodriver is in
$PATH). The Trisquel-native way to run the automated tests without any of
this is to load the extension as a Temporary Add-on in your browser (see
TEST above); that is the recommended path.

With selenium-webdriver and geckodriver available, you can:

Run the test suite headlessly (build the xpi WITH the test suite, ./build.sh -t):
  $ node ./utilities/test.js
  $ node ./utilities/test.js 12345      # optionally, with a chosen seed

Check a webpage for compliance (build the xpi WITHOUT the test suite):
  $ node ./utilities/compliance.js <url>

For example, to check the compliance of the FSF homepage, do
  $ node ./utilities/compliance.js https://fsf.org

It will open the url in a headless browser, save a screenshot, and
output the compliance check result.


